<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#hadramautic-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hadramautic language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hadramitic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hadramautic was one of the Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabian</a> languages, written in the Kingdom of Hadramawt in what is now eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> from the early first millennium BCE until about the 4th century CE. It used the South Arabian (Musnad) script and is known from inscriptions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabwa">Shabwa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumhuram">Sumhuram</a> and other sites. It differs from Sabaic in features such as the form of its third-person pronouns.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hadramautic-language.jpg" alt="Hadramautic language" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tiwahi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hadramawt_400_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
